1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,027
Open a project's history and it looks like a tangled ball of string.

2
00:00:05,027 --> 00:00:05,427
Merge,

3
00:00:05,427 --> 00:00:05,826
merge,

4
00:00:05,826 --> 00:00:06,225
merge,

5
00:00:06,225 --> 00:00:08,223
and nobody can read the story.

6
00:00:08,773 --> 00:00:10,764
Last lesson you merged branches.

7
00:00:10,764 --> 00:00:13,315
Today you learn the other tool: rebasing,

8
00:00:13,315 --> 00:00:16,426
which replays your work on top of the latest code,

9
00:00:16,426 --> 00:00:18,479
so history stays a straight line.

10
00:00:19,029 --> 00:00:19,899
Three skills.

11
00:00:19,899 --> 00:00:20,300
Amend,

12
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:21,905
to fix your last commit.

13
00:00:21,905 --> 00:00:22,373
Rebase,

14
00:00:22,373 --> 00:00:24,447
to move a branch onto new work.

15
00:00:24,447 --> 00:00:25,851
And rebase conflicts,

16
00:00:25,851 --> 00:00:28,928
which are just merge conflicts in slow motion.

17
00:00:29,478 --> 00:00:32,298
And one golden rule that keeps you out of trouble.

18
00:00:32,298 --> 00:00:32,862
As always,

19
00:00:32,862 --> 00:00:35,174
there is a lab with a checker at the end.

20
00:00:37,224 --> 00:00:38,593
Here is the situation.

21
00:00:38,593 --> 00:00:40,025
You branched from main,

22
00:00:40,025 --> 00:00:41,269
and did two commits.

23
00:00:41,269 --> 00:00:41,892
Meanwhile,

24
00:00:41,892 --> 00:00:43,821
someone added a commit to main.

25
00:00:43,821 --> 00:00:45,501
The branches have diverged.

26
00:00:46,051 --> 00:00:48,534
Merging joins them with a merge commit.

27
00:00:48,534 --> 00:00:48,852
Safe,

28
00:00:48,852 --> 00:00:51,271
and it keeps the real order of events,

29
00:00:51,271 --> 00:00:53,945
but the history now has a fork and a join.

30
00:00:54,495 --> 00:00:56,578
Rebasing does something different.

31
00:00:56,578 --> 00:00:57,987
Git takes your commits,

32
00:00:57,987 --> 00:00:58,967
sets them aside,

33
00:00:58,967 --> 00:01:01,234
moves your branch to the tip of main,

34
00:01:01,234 --> 00:01:03,194
and replays your commits on top,

35
00:01:03,194 --> 00:01:04,052
one at a time.

36
00:01:04,602 --> 00:01:06,321
The result is a straight line,

37
00:01:06,321 --> 00:01:08,498
as if you had started your work today.

38
00:01:08,498 --> 00:01:09,701
But there is a catch.

39
00:01:10,251 --> 00:01:11,700
Your commits are copied.

40
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:13,330
They have the same changes,

41
00:01:13,330 --> 00:01:14,840
but they are new commits,

42
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:15,806
with new hashes.

43
00:01:15,806 --> 00:01:17,376
Rebasing rewrites history.

44
00:01:17,926 --> 00:01:19,891
Remember this: rebase rewrites.

45
00:01:19,891 --> 00:01:21,095
Amend rewrites too.

46
00:01:21,095 --> 00:01:24,328
So everything we do today applies to your own work,

47
00:01:24,328 --> 00:01:26,737
not to work other people already have.

48
00:01:28,787 --> 00:01:32,452
First the smallest rewrite: fixing the last commit.

49
00:01:32,452 --> 00:01:36,118
This project has a last commit with a lazy message.

50
00:01:36,118 --> 00:01:36,549
Go in.

51
00:01:36,549 --> 00:01:37,196
Type: CD,

52
00:01:37,196 --> 00:01:37,555
site.

53
00:01:38,105 --> 00:01:41,860
Look at the history. Type: git log, dash dash one line.

54
00:01:42,410 --> 00:01:44,007
The top commit says wip.

55
00:01:44,007 --> 00:01:46,735
Work in progress tells your team nothing.

56
00:01:46,735 --> 00:01:47,466
Rewrite it.

57
00:01:47,466 --> 00:01:48,598
Type: git commit,

58
00:01:48,598 --> 00:01:49,662
dash dash amend,

59
00:01:49,662 --> 00:01:50,128
dash M,

60
00:01:50,128 --> 00:01:50,527
quote,

61
00:01:50,527 --> 00:01:51,525
Add owner line,

62
00:01:51,525 --> 00:01:51,924
quote.

63
00:01:52,474 --> 00:01:55,593
Amend replaces the last commit with a new one.

64
00:01:55,593 --> 00:01:56,339
Look again.

65
00:01:56,339 --> 00:01:57,288
Type: git log,

66
00:01:57,288 --> 00:01:58,576
dash dash one line.

67
00:01:59,126 --> 00:02:00,043
Same position,

68
00:02:00,043 --> 00:02:01,025
better message,

69
00:02:01,025 --> 00:02:02,401
and the hash changed.

70
00:02:02,401 --> 00:02:06,135
That is the proof that a new commit replaced the old one.

71
00:02:06,135 --> 00:02:07,052
Forgot a file?

72
00:02:07,052 --> 00:02:07,641
Stage it,

73
00:02:07,641 --> 00:02:10,196
then amend with dash dash no dash edit,

74
00:02:10,196 --> 00:02:12,096
and it joins the last commit.

75
00:02:12,096 --> 00:02:14,912
Only amend commits you have not shared yet.

76
00:02:16,962 --> 00:02:18,310
Now a real rebase.

77
00:02:18,310 --> 00:02:19,508
In this project,

78
00:02:19,508 --> 00:02:23,401
you are on a feature branch that diverged from main.

79
00:02:23,401 --> 00:02:23,850
Go in.

80
00:02:23,850 --> 00:02:24,524
Type: CD,

81
00:02:24,524 --> 00:02:24,898
site.

82
00:02:25,448 --> 00:02:31,229
Look at the picture. Type: git log, dash dash one line, dash dash graph, dash dash all.

83
00:02:31,779 --> 00:02:34,932
Main has one commit that your branch does not have,

84
00:02:34,932 --> 00:02:37,900
and your branch has two that main does not have.

85
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:39,940
Now rebase your branch onto main.

86
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:41,424
You stay on your branch,

87
00:02:41,424 --> 00:02:42,537
and name the base.

88
00:02:42,537 --> 00:02:43,588
Type: git rebase,

89
00:02:43,588 --> 00:02:43,897
main.

90
00:02:44,447 --> 00:02:45,909
Successfully rebased.

91
00:02:45,909 --> 00:02:47,998
Git replayed your two commits.

92
00:02:47,998 --> 00:02:49,809
Look at the picture again.

93
00:02:49,809 --> 00:02:50,784
Type: git log,

94
00:02:50,784 --> 00:02:52,107
dash dash one line,

95
00:02:52,107 --> 00:02:53,221
dash dash graph,

96
00:02:53,221 --> 00:02:54,196
dash dash all.

97
00:02:54,746 --> 00:02:55,938
One straight line.

98
00:02:55,938 --> 00:02:58,519
Notice the two commits have new hashes.

99
00:02:58,519 --> 00:02:59,711
Now bring it home.

100
00:02:59,711 --> 00:03:00,836
Type: git switch,

101
00:03:00,836 --> 00:03:01,167
main.

102
00:03:01,717 --> 00:03:05,131
On main. Merge the feature. Type: git merge, feature.

103
00:03:05,681 --> 00:03:06,562
Fast forward!

104
00:03:06,562 --> 00:03:09,883
Because the branch now sits right on top of main,

105
00:03:09,883 --> 00:03:11,780
there is nothing to combine,

106
00:03:11,780 --> 00:03:13,813
so Git just moves the pointer.

107
00:03:13,813 --> 00:03:14,627
Final check.

108
00:03:14,627 --> 00:03:15,575
Type: git log,

109
00:03:15,575 --> 00:03:16,863
dash dash one line,

110
00:03:16,863 --> 00:03:17,947
dash dash graph.

111
00:03:18,497 --> 00:03:23,639
A perfectly linear history, with no merge commit. That is why teams love rebase.

112
00:03:25,689 --> 00:03:27,429
Rebase can conflict too,

113
00:03:27,429 --> 00:03:28,589
just like merge.

114
00:03:28,589 --> 00:03:31,343
Here both sides changed the port line.

115
00:03:31,343 --> 00:03:31,778
Go in.

116
00:03:31,778 --> 00:03:32,431
Type: CD,

117
00:03:32,431 --> 00:03:32,793
site.

118
00:03:33,343 --> 00:03:36,287
Rebase onto main. Type: git rebase, main.

119
00:03:36,837 --> 00:03:37,441
Conflict,

120
00:03:37,441 --> 00:03:38,515
in app dot conf.

121
00:03:38,515 --> 00:03:41,133
Git replays your commits one at a time,

122
00:03:41,133 --> 00:03:44,288
and stopped at the first one that does not fit.

123
00:03:44,288 --> 00:03:45,765
Ask Git where you are.

124
00:03:45,765 --> 00:03:46,906
Type: git status.

125
00:03:47,456 --> 00:03:50,200
Look at the first line: rebase in progress,

126
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:52,370
and the hint tells you what to do.

127
00:03:52,370 --> 00:03:53,263
Open the file.

128
00:03:53,263 --> 00:03:53,901
Type: cat,

129
00:03:53,901 --> 00:03:54,731
app dot conf.

130
00:03:55,281 --> 00:03:55,788
Careful,

131
00:03:55,788 --> 00:03:57,943
in a rebase the sides are swapped.

132
00:03:57,943 --> 00:03:58,767
Head is main,

133
00:03:58,767 --> 00:04:00,731
the code you are rebasing onto.

134
00:04:00,731 --> 00:04:03,837
The other side is your own commit being replayed.

135
00:04:03,837 --> 00:04:06,499
The team wants port eight thousand eighty.

136
00:04:06,499 --> 00:04:07,829
Write the final file.

137
00:04:07,829 --> 00:04:08,653
Type: printf,

138
00:04:08,653 --> 00:04:10,301
the text in single quotes,

139
00:04:10,301 --> 00:04:12,456
port equals eight thousand eighty,

140
00:04:12,456 --> 00:04:13,216
backslash N,

141
00:04:13,216 --> 00:04:14,484
timeout equals five,

142
00:04:14,484 --> 00:04:15,245
backslash N,

143
00:04:15,245 --> 00:04:16,956
redirect into app dot conf.

144
00:04:17,506 --> 00:04:20,258
Stage it. Type: git add, app dot conf.

145
00:04:20,808 --> 00:04:25,224
Now tell Git to carry on. Type: git rebase, dash dash continue.

146
00:04:25,774 --> 00:04:29,514
Git may open your editor to confirm the commit message.

147
00:04:29,514 --> 00:04:31,079
Just save and close it.

148
00:04:31,079 --> 00:04:34,751
In this recording that step is accepted automatically.

149
00:04:34,751 --> 00:04:37,132
Your second commit applied cleanly.

150
00:04:37,132 --> 00:04:38,356
Check the history.

151
00:04:38,356 --> 00:04:39,308
Type: git log,

152
00:04:39,308 --> 00:04:40,600
dash dash one line,

153
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:41,688
dash dash graph.

154
00:04:42,238 --> 00:04:43,598
A straight line again.

155
00:04:43,598 --> 00:04:45,637
And if you ever panic mid rebase,

156
00:04:45,637 --> 00:04:50,025
git rebase dash dash abort takes you back to exactly where you started.

157
00:04:52,175 --> 00:04:56,527
Now the golden rule. Never rebase commits that other people already have.

158
00:04:57,077 --> 00:04:58,845
Rebasing creates new commits.

159
00:04:58,845 --> 00:05:01,589
If a teammate already built on your old ones,

160
00:05:01,589 --> 00:05:04,028
their history and yours no longer match,

161
00:05:04,028 --> 00:05:05,674
and fixing that is painful.

162
00:05:06,224 --> 00:05:09,419
So: rebase your own local branch before you share it,

163
00:05:09,419 --> 00:05:11,168
to keep it clean and current.

164
00:05:11,168 --> 00:05:13,338
Once it is pushed and others use it,

165
00:05:13,338 --> 00:05:14,182
merge instead.

166
00:05:14,732 --> 00:05:15,725
A simple guide.

167
00:05:15,725 --> 00:05:16,586
Private work,

168
00:05:16,586 --> 00:05:17,579
rebase is fine.

169
00:05:17,579 --> 00:05:18,374
Shared work,

170
00:05:18,374 --> 00:05:18,771
merge.

171
00:05:18,771 --> 00:05:19,963
And when in doubt,

172
00:05:19,963 --> 00:05:20,360
merge,

173
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:23,009
because merging never rewrites anything.

174
00:05:25,059 --> 00:05:27,832
Your task, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

175
00:05:28,382 --> 00:05:30,529
The lab is lab sixty dot S H,

176
00:05:30,529 --> 00:05:34,303
in the academy slash lab folder of git hub dot com,

177
00:05:34,303 --> 00:05:35,783
slash it dash waves,

178
00:05:35,783 --> 00:05:37,485
slash dev ops dash A I.

179
00:05:37,485 --> 00:05:39,188
Install it like before,

180
00:05:39,188 --> 00:05:40,372
then run lab up.

181
00:05:40,922 --> 00:05:45,135
It builds a repository with a feature branch that diverged from main.

182
00:05:45,135 --> 00:05:47,578
The last commit on the feature says wip.

183
00:05:48,128 --> 00:05:51,861
One: on the feature branch, amend the last commit to a clear message.

184
00:05:52,411 --> 00:05:54,886
Two: rebase the feature branch onto main.

185
00:05:55,436 --> 00:05:57,939
Three: switch to main and merge the feature,

186
00:05:57,939 --> 00:05:59,361
so main has all the work,

187
00:05:59,361 --> 00:06:00,556
with no merge commit.

188
00:06:01,106 --> 00:06:05,650
Four: in answers dot text, write one number: how many commits main has now.

189
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:11,640
Then run lab check. Four pass lines. Remember: only rebase your own unshared work.

190
00:06:13,690 --> 00:06:15,674
Six points, and you need all six.

191
00:06:16,224 --> 00:06:18,059
The checker shows four pass lines.

192
00:06:18,609 --> 00:06:21,531
The history is a straight line, with no merge commit.

193
00:06:22,081 --> 00:06:24,641
The last commit has a clear message, not wip.

194
00:06:25,191 --> 00:06:28,007
You can explain why rebasing changes the hashes.

195
00:06:28,557 --> 00:06:30,883
The number in answers dot text is correct.

196
00:06:31,433 --> 00:06:33,075
You ran lab down to clean up.

197
00:06:33,625 --> 00:06:35,247
To pass, you need all six.

198
00:06:37,297 --> 00:06:40,134
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

199
00:06:40,684 --> 00:06:42,903
One: install the lab, and run lab up.

200
00:06:43,453 --> 00:06:45,437
Two: amend the last commit message.

201
00:06:45,987 --> 00:06:48,526
Three: rebase the feature branch onto main.

202
00:06:49,076 --> 00:06:51,572
Four: switch to main, and merge the feature.

203
00:06:52,122 --> 00:06:55,492
Five: count the commits, write the number, and run lab check.

204
00:06:56,042 --> 00:06:57,514
Six: run lab down.

205
00:06:58,064 --> 00:06:59,899
Pause the video, and begin now.

206
00:07:15,449 --> 00:07:16,324
Quick check.

207
00:07:16,874 --> 00:07:18,730
What does rebase do to your commits?

208
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:21,456
Why does the hash change after an amend?

209
00:07:22,006 --> 00:07:23,798
Which commits are safe to rebase?

210
00:07:24,348 --> 00:07:25,948
How do you back out of a rebase?

211
00:07:26,498 --> 00:07:31,148
Objective covered: M C zero seven, objective four, rebasing.

212
00:07:31,698 --> 00:07:33,768
